// GitHub issue #447: the second core stops parking and becomes an idle
// execution context -- interrupts enabled at its OWN GIC CPU interface,
// taking its OWN timer tick, and in `wfi` the rest of the time.
//
// What core 1 does NOT do here is enter the scheduler. That is the next
// issue, and the separation is what lets this one land without moving
// KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES: this core executes kernel code, but it reaches no
// kernel STATE, so the three legs in kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb still
// hold and the ten static_asserts that name them still say something true.
// The constant's own comment moved from "core 1 parks" to that stricter
// claim in the same change, because "it parks" stopped being why.
//
// Why the per-core work exists at all: GICC_PMR/GICC_CTLR are the GICv2
// CPU INTERFACE and are banked per core, so core 0's one write does not
// reach core 1 -- a core that never writes them has interrupts disabled at
// its own interface no matter what the distributor says. The timer is the
// same twice over: GICD_ISENABLER0 bit 30 is a PPI, which the GIC banks per
// CPU (kernel/platform/*/timer_irq.tkb's own comment already said so, for a
// different reason), and CNTP_TVAL_EL0/CNTP_CTL_EL0 are per-core registers
// by construction. None of it is new code; what was missing was a path on
// the secondary core that reached it.
//
// Current limitations:
// - ONE secondary core, by decision of 2026-08-27: every class of race
//   appears at two, and four adds reduced reproducibility plus a different
//   problem (scalability) to a phase that is finding correctness bugs.
//   SECONDARY_CORE_ID is that decision, not a hardware limit.
// - The idle core reads and writes exactly one word of shared memory, its
//   own tick counter, through atomics. It takes no lock because it enters
//   no locked subsystem; if it ever does, that stops being true and
//   kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb's leg 3 is what to reread.
// - The core's exception stacks are its own as of GitHub issue #477. That
//   was not true when this file was first written, and the first timer
//   tick here landed on core 0's saved frame -- its INTID came back as
//   core 0's ELR. Nothing in THIS file could have prevented that, which is
//   why it is a separate issue and not a caveat here.
// - The tick counter is evidence, not a clock. It counts this core's timer
//   interrupts and says nothing about wall time.
// - Nothing here schedules. A process cannot run on core 1 yet.

use "kernel/lib/execution_model.tkb";

// Which core PSCI starts. Two cores, not four.
const SECONDARY_CORE_ID: usize = 1;

// The handshake word core 0 polls to know the secondary reached compiled
// Takibi. `io` rather than an atomic because that is what it has always
// been and it is verified on both boards; it is a one-shot publication with
// a single writer and a bounded poll, not a value read repeatedly.
private let mut kernel_secondary_boot_state: io usize;

// This core's timer-interrupt count. Written only by the secondary core,
// from its interrupt handler; read only by core 0, for a view.
//
// Atomic rather than a plain global, and this is the first place in this
// kernel where that is load-bearing rather than defensive: the two accesses
// genuinely run on different cores at the same time, which is the exact
// situation `io` (volatile, no ordering) does not cover. One writer means
// no compare-and-swap is needed -- see GitHub issue #450 for why there
// isn't one to reach for anyway.
private let mut secondary_tick_count: usize;

private fn secondary_tick_address() -> usize {
    return (&secondary_tick_count) as usize;
}

fn kernel_secondary_boot_state_clear() {
    kernel_secondary_boot_state = 0;
}

fn kernel_secondary_boot_state_value() -> usize {
    return kernel_secondary_boot_state;
}

// Read by core 0 for the view. The acquire pairs with the handler's own
// release-free fetch-add on the same word: what is being ordered is this
// core's view of a counter another core is incrementing, and the only
// claim made is that the value read was one this core actually reached.
fn secondary_tick_value() -> usize !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { atomic_load_acquire(secondary_tick_address()) };
}

// Called from a platform IRQ dispatch when the interrupted core is not core
// 0 and the interrupt is the timer PPI. Rearms this core's own timer and
// counts. Deliberately does NOT call kernel_process_net_wake_all() or
// request a reschedule the way the core-0 path does -- both reach kernel
// state, and this core reaching none of it is the property that keeps
// KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES at 1.
fn secondary_timer_tick() !{interrupt, unsafe} {
    timer_tick_rearm();
    unsafe { atomic_fetch_add_relaxed(secondary_tick_address(), 1); }
}

// Reached only by the secondary core, after PSCI has installed the entry
// point, that entry has selected its private stack, and the shared EL1 page
// table is active. The context value prevents a coincidental stale write
// from passing.
//
// One copy, in arch/, for the reason scripts/check_platform_file_parity.py
// exists: the two platform trees are never compiled together, so two
// identical copies of this would be free to drift with nothing able to
// notice. What is genuinely per-platform is behind cpu_id() and
// platform_irq_cpu_interface_init() -- the GICC addresses -- which is
// where the difference belongs.
//
// Does not return. The `wfi` loop IS the idle context: interrupts are
// enabled, the vector table is this core's, and the handler runs on this
// core's own stack. `wfi` and not interrupt_wait()'s `wfe` because a core
// waiting on wfe is woken by any core's SEV and core 0 executes one on
// every tick -- the whole point is that this core wakes for its own
// interrupt and nothing else.
fn kernel_secondary_main(context: usize) {
    // GitHub issue #453's worklist, joined deliberately. This core runs
    // kernel code and touches no kernel state, which is the ONLY reason
    // KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES can stay 1 while it does. Raising the constant
    // means core 1 gets an ExecutionState slot and may schedule -- at
    // which point the loop below stops being an idle loop and this
    // function is one of the places that has to change.
    static_assert(KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES == 1,
                  "the secondary core idles and reaches no kernel state: give it a scheduler before giving it a slot");
    let core_id: usize = cpu_id();
    if (context == 0x191 && core_id == SECONDARY_CORE_ID) {
        kernel_secondary_boot_state = 0x100 + core_id;
        signal_fence();
        // Everything a core has to do FOR ITSELF, in the order it has to
        // do it. Core 0 reaches the same three through its platform's
        // platform_irq_init(), which additionally does the distributor and
        // the device routing -- those belong to the machine, not to a
        // core. Composing them is platform-independent, so it lives here
        // rather than in two files that could drift
        // (scripts/check_platform_file_parity.py caught exactly that when
        // this was first written as a per-platform helper).
        platform_irq_cpu_interface_init();
        timer_irq_setup();
        enable_irq();
        while (true) {
            // GitHub issue #445: the two-core contention probe, driven
            // from here rather than from the tick, so both cores are
            // inside the same lock at the same time instead of taking
            // turns a timer period apart. It touches ONE pool, this
            // kernel's only pool that no kernel subsystem uses, and it
            // never reaches the page allocator -- see that file's floor.
            if (pool_contention_is_armed()) {
                pool_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            // GitHub issue #479 Group A: the same arrangement, one
            // subsystem over. Each probe owns its own instance, so a
            // failure names the primitive rather than whatever else shares
            // a live one.
            if (freelist_contention_is_armed()) {
                freelist_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            if (page_contention_is_armed()) {
                page_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            if (asid_contention_is_armed()) {
                asid_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            if (fd_refcount_contention_is_armed()) {
                fd_refcount_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            if (pid_contention_is_armed()) {
                pid_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            if (tag_contention_is_armed()) {
                tag_contention_secondary_run();
            }
            wfi();
        }
    }
    // Falls through to the entry path's own park for any core that is not
    // the one PSCI was asked to start, or a context that does not match.
    // That is a "this should not have happened" path, not an idle one.
}

// Does the secondary core's tick advance while core 0 CANNOT take an
// interrupt at all? That is the independence claim, asked in the one way a
// single boot can answer it deterministically: core 0 masks its own IRQs,
// spins on the physical counter for a fixed window, and compares the
// secondary's counter across it.
//
// Masking is what makes this evidence rather than a coincidence. With
// interrupts enabled on both cores, a counter that advanced would prove
// only that time passed. With core 0 unable to take even its own timer,
// anything the secondary counts came from the secondary's own interface
// and its own timer -- which is exactly the per-core banking this issue is
// about.
//
// The window is a fraction of a second derived from CNTFRQ_EL0 rather than
// a cycle count, because the two boards' counters run at different
// frequencies (54 MHz on RPi5) and a fixed spin count would be a different
// duration on each. The secondary's tick period is CNTFRQ >> 6, so a
// window of CNTFRQ >> 4 covers several ticks with margin.
fn secondary_ticks_independent() -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let window: i64 = read_cntfrq() >> 4;
    disable_irq();
    let before: usize = secondary_tick_value();
    let start: i64 = read_cntpct();
    while (read_cntpct() - start < window) { }
    let after: usize = secondary_tick_value();
    enable_irq();
    return after > before;
}
